      "dom_id": "question-13844",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {(-{u}^{-1})|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{u})|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
