      "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
