      "problem": "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | ${e}^{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
